**Sec. 100001.** This section establishes that the immigration fees detailed in Subtitle A apply to aliens as defined in the Immigration and Nationality Act. It mandates that all terms used in this subtitle carry the meanings assigned to them in Section 101 of the Immigration and Nationality Act. Furthermore, any reference to a section or provision within this subtitle is explicitly construed as a reference to the corresponding section or provision of the Immigration and Nationality Act.

**Sec. 100002.** This section imposes a non-waivable asylum application fee, set at a minimum of $100 for fiscal year 2025, which adjusts annually for inflation. The fee is collected by the Secretary of Homeland Security or the Attorney General at the time the asylum application is filed. Proceeds are split evenly, with 50 percent credited to the Executive Office for Immigration Review and 50 percent to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, both of which may retain and spend these funds without further appropriation.

**Sec. 100003.** This section establishes non-waivable fees for initial employment authorization documents for asylum applicants, parolees, and individuals with Temporary Protected Status, each set at a minimum of $550 for fiscal year 2025. For asylum applicants, 25 percent of collected fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services for fraud detection, while the remainder goes to the Treasury. For parolees and Temporary Protected Status holders, all collected fees are deposited directly into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100004.** This section imposes a non-waivable $1,000 fee on aliens paroled into the United States, with exceptions for medical emergencies, organ donation, imminent family death, and specific law enforcement assistance cases. The fee amount adjusts annually for inflation, and all collected proceeds are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury. The Secretary of Homeland Security may establish a higher fee by rule, but cannot waive or reduce the fee except for the specified exceptions.

**Sec. 100005.** This section requires aliens, parents, or legal guardians applying for special immigrant juvenile status to pay a non-waivable fee of at least $250 in fiscal year 2025. The fee amount is subject to annual inflation adjustments based on the Consumer Price Index. All fees collected under this section are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100006.** This section amends the Immigration and Nationality Act to increase the maximum fee for Temporary Protected Status applications from $50 to $500. The new fee is subject to annual inflation adjustments and cannot be waived or reduced. All fees collected under this provision are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100007.** This section imposes a non-waivable $250 fee on aliens issued a nonimmigrant visa, which adjusts annually for inflation. The Secretary of Homeland Security may reimburse this fee if the alien complies with all visa conditions and either departs the United States within five days of the visa's expiration or adjusts to lawful permanent resident status. Unreimbursed fees are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100008.** This section imposes a non-waivable $24 fee on aliens submitting an application for a Form I-94 Arrival/Departure Record, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty percent of the collected fees are deposited into the Land Border Inspection Fee Account for U.S. Customs and Border Protection to retain and spend on Form I-94 processing. The remaining 80 percent of the fees are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100009.** This section requires aliens with pending asylum applications to pay a non-waivable annual fee of at least $100 for each calendar year the application remains pending. The fee amount adjusts annually for inflation based on the Consumer Price Index. All fees collected under this section are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100010.** This section imposes a non-waivable $275 fee on parolees seeking renewal or extension of employment authorization, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty-five percent of the collected fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services for retention and expenditure without further appropriation. The remaining 75 percent of the fees are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100011.** This section imposes a non-waivable fee of at least $275 on asylum applicants for each renewal or extension of employment authorization. The section also specifies that employment authorization terminates immediately upon denial of the asylum application by an immigration officer or judge, unless an appeal is filed. Twenty-five percent of the collected fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, while the remainder goes to the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100012.** This section imposes a non-waivable $275 fee on aliens with Temporary Protected Status seeking renewal or extension of employment authorization, with annual inflation adjustments. Twenty-five percent of the collected fees are credited to U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services for retention and expenditure without further appropriation. The remaining 75 percent of the fees are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100013.** This section establishes various non-waivable fees for immigration court proceedings, including $1,500 for adjustment of status, $1,050 for waivers of inadmissibility, $500 for Temporary Protected Status, $900 for appeals and motions, and $600 for cancellation of removal. Not more than 25 percent of these fees are transferred to the Executive Office for Immigration Review for retention and spending, with the remainder going to the Treasury. The Executive Office for Immigration Review is prohibited from using these funds for the Legal Orientation Program.

**Sec. 100014.** This section amends the Electronic System for Travel Authorization fee structure to require a fee of not less than $13 per travel authorization, in addition to existing fees. The authorization for this fee collection is extended from October 31, 2028, to October 31, 2034. The fee amount is subject to annual inflation adjustments based on the Consumer Price Index.

**Sec. 100015.** This section imposes a non-waivable $30 fee on aliens enrolling in the Electronic Visa Update System, with annual inflation adjustments. A new CBP Electronic Visa Update System Account is established in the Treasury, receiving the difference between total fees collected and $5 per payment. The $5 per payment portion is deposited into the general fund of the Treasury, while the account funds are appropriated for U.S. Customs and Border Protection program costs.

**Sec. 100016.** This section imposes a non-waivable $5,000 fee on aliens ordered removed in absentia who are subsequently arrested by U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement, with annual inflation adjustments. Fifty percent of the collected fees are credited to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for retention and expenditure without further appropriation. The remaining 50 percent of the fees are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100017.** This section imposes a non-waivable $5,000 fee on inadmissible aliens apprehended between ports of entry, with annual inflation adjustments. Fifty percent of the collected fees are credited to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for retention and expenditure without further appropriation. The remaining 50 percent of the fees are deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 100018.** This section amends the Immigration and Nationality Act to change the language regarding asylum application fees from permissive to mandatory. It removes the cap on fees, stating that nothing limits the Attorney General's authority to set additional adjudication and naturalization fees in accordance with existing law.

**Sec. 100051.** This section appropriates $2,055,000,000 to the Department of Homeland Security for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are designated for hiring and training Customs and Border Protection agents, transportation for removals, background checks, DNA collection, and state and local participation in immigration enforcement. Specific allocations include funding for the removal of specified unaccompanied alien children and expedited removal of criminal aliens.

**Sec. 100052.** This section appropriates $29,850,000,000 to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds support hiring and training personnel, performance and retention bonuses, transportation for removals, facility upgrades, and fleet modernization. Additional funding is provided for family unity measures, 287(g) agreements, the Victims of Immigration Crime Engagement Office, and the Office of the Principal Legal Advisor.

**Sec. 100053.** This section appropriates $750,000,000 to the Federal Law Enforcement Training Centers for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029. At least $285,000,000 is designated for training newly hired federal, state, and local law enforcement personnel. No more than $465,000,000 is allocated for facility procurement, construction, maintenance, and equipment.

**Sec. 100054.** This section appropriates $3,330,000,000 to the Department of Justice for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. Funds are allocated for hiring immigration judges (capped at 800 by November 1, 2028), combating drug trafficking, prosecuting immigration and gang crimes, and supporting the Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Grant Program. Additional funds compensate states for incarcerating criminal aliens, provided the states do not restrict cooperation with federal immigration enforcement.

**Sec. 100055.** This section establishes the Bridging Immigration-related Deficits Experienced Nationwide (BIDEN) Reimbursement Fund within the Department of Justice. It appropriates up to $3,500,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2028, for grants to states and local governments. These grants cover costs for apprehending criminal aliens, investigating crimes, court operations, and transporting aliens, with eligibility for expenses incurred on or after January 20, 2021.

**Sec. 100056.** This section appropriates $5,000,000,000 to the Bureau of Prisons for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. At least $3,000,000,000 is designated for hiring and training new employees and funding salaries and benefits for the current workforce. No more than $2,000,000,000 is allocated for maintenance and repairs to Bureau of Prisons facilities.

**Sec. 100057.** This section appropriates $1,170,000,000 to the United States Secret Service for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are restricted to additional resources, including personnel, training, and technology, as well as performance, retention, and signing bonuses. Retention bonuses require a two-year service commitment, while signing bonuses require a five-year service commitment.

**Sec. 100101.** This section appropriates $1,250,000 annually to the Administrative Office of the United States Courts for fiscal years 2025 through 2028. The funds are designated for analyzing court dockets and preparing statistical reports, specifically assessing the number and cost impact of judicial orders issuing non-party relief against the Federal Government.

**Sec. 100102.** This section appropriates $1,000,000 annually to the Federal Judicial Center for fiscal years 2025 through 2028. The funds are used for continuing education and training programs for judicial branch personnel. Specific training topics include the lack of authority for non-party relief against the Federal Government and strategies to mitigate the cost impact of such claims on taxpayers.

**Sec. 100201.** This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to extend the termination date of the Radiation Exposure Compensation Fund. The Fund, which previously had a different expiration, is now set to terminate on December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 100202.** This section expands eligibility for Radiation Exposure Compensation Act claims related to atmospheric testing by extending the covered period to November 6, 1962, and adding New Mexico as an affected area for certain claims. It increases the compensation amount for leukemia and specified disease claims to $100,000. The section also defines "affected area" to include specific counties in Nevada, Arizona, and the states of New Mexico, Utah, and Idaho.

**Sec. 100203.** This section expands Radiation Exposure Compensation Act eligibility for uranium miners, millers, and ore transporters by extending the covered period to December 31, 1990, and adding core drillers and remediation workers. It adds renal cancer and other chronic renal diseases to the list of compensable conditions. The section also allows individuals with combined work histories in multiple positions to qualify if they meet specific exposure or duration requirements.

**Sec. 100204.** This section adds a new provision to the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act for claims relating to Manhattan Project waste. It provides compensation to individuals who were physically present in specified affected areas in Missouri, Tennessee, Alaska, and Kentucky for at least two years after January 1, 1949, and contracted a specified disease. Living claimants receive at least $50,000 plus documented out-of-pocket medical expenses, while beneficiaries of deceased claimants receive $25,000.

**Sec. 100205.** This section amends the Radiation Exposure Compensation Act to change the deadline for filing claims. The previous deadline of two years after the enactment of the RECA Extension Act of 2022 is replaced with a fixed deadline of December 31, 2027. [end of text]


